statement:
Let $p, q, b$ be real numbers with $1 \le p \le q \le 2$ and $0 \le b \le 1$, and set $\rho = \sqrt{(p-1)/(q-1)}$. Then
\[
  \frac{(1+\rho b)^q + (1-\rho b)^q - 2}{q}
  \;\le\;
  \frac{(1+b)^p + (1-b)^p - 2}{p}.
\]

replaced:
Let $1 \le p \le q \le 2$, $b \in [0,1]$ and $\rho = \sqrt{(p-1)/(q-1)}$. Then
\[
  \frac{(1+\rho b)^q + (1-\rho b)^q - 2}{q}
  \;\le\;
  \frac{(1+b)^p + (1-b)^p - 2}{p}.
\]
